{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1002578-onmyoji-where-are-they-now","id":1002578,"title":"Onmyoji: Where Are They Now?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2016-11-13T11:34:35-08:00","id":1002578,"title":"Onmyoji: Where Are They Now?","content":"I think we can all agree that, between their Western viewpoint and their unwillingness to invalidate earlier canon, White Wolf has always had its blind spots in terms of magical traditions. (I still haven't gotten around to starting that thread on the past and current magics of Central American natives). One of these blind spots is the curious exclusion of Japanese <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Onmy%C5%8Dd%C5%8D\" target=\"_blank\">Onmyodo</a>, and its practitioners, the Onmyoji.<br /> <br /> Short explanation, Onmyodo is a syncreticism between Toaist and Buddhist beliefs imported from China, Astrology from India, and Japan's native Shinto animism. All formalized upon the Imperial Japanese government's creation of civil positions for Onmyoji. And yes, Shintoism is brought up in numerous books, and most Shinto Mages being neatly shuffled into either the Akashayana or the Dreamspeakers. But the art and science of Onmyodo is a special case, as it adds an element normally overlooked in &quot;normal&quot; Buddhist-Shinto Magery: High Ritual Magick.<br /> <br /> So it begs the question: after more recent centuries disbanded Onmyoji departments for their superstitious ways (and then modern times increased this sentiment), where did the Onmyoji go?<br /> <br /> There are three possibilities I can see from this, each with their own pros and cons. (None of these are that they joined the Five Elemental Dragons, as they are scientists, not true mystics).<br /> <br /> One, they followed standard Buddhist monks into either the Akashayana or Dreamspeakers, blending in with the mass. Akashayana members would have the advantage of being able to join a Faction - the Shi Ren - whose methods are not too disimilar to their own. And a Dreamspeaker Onmyoji could just become a monk, as the Buddhist religious establishment retains a modicum of standing in Japan even to this day. Of course, either of these choices sort of dilute the unique system the Onmyoji practiced.<br /> <br /> Two, the Onmyoji could go independent, spinning the Bureau of Onmyo off into an extra-governmental Craft. Despite Japan rejecting their methods, they could remain loyal to its greater good. Thus, working in the shadows to divine potential disasters and protect the innocent from recalcitrant ghosts. The problem here is that I can't see the group being terribly large, so it becomes questionable that they managed to survive government and Technocrat purges to the modern day, while acting alone.<br /> <br /> Three, the Onmyoji ally themselves with a different group: the Wu Lung.<br /> <br /> Consider that many of the methods Onmyodo imported from China were the exact same methods the Wu Lung base their entire methodology around. Toaism, Buddhism, Confucianism. The Five Chinese Elements and the concepts of Yin and Yang. Even the fact that the Onmyoji held a place in their local imperial government is very reminiscent of the very imperial Wu Lung. Only the addition of Shintoism is a major sticking point, and even then, the Wu Lung are spiritualists and animists after a fashion; they just believe in the idea of all Shen being part of the 10,000 Things, governed by the Celestial Bureaucracy. As long as the Onmyoji agreed to pay respects to the August Personage of Jade and work within the systems of civil exams, they might slide in as a weird adjunct to the Wu Lung. And between the Wu Lung's fall from grace and today, they've hardly been in a position to refuse aid of a half-way decent variety (by Wu Lung standards of course).<br /> <br /> The major difficulties of this come from the fact that the Wu Lung are Chinese and the Onmyoji Japanese. This becomes more than a little awkward in modern times, given the animosity between China and Japan over the whole WWII, Rape of Nanking thing. And the Wu Lung, being elitists and racists, might balk at the idea of lowering themselves to taking members from outside China. It took forever for them to even consider taking applicants that were not pure blooded Chinese.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":6156,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","user":"Bluecho","date":". 11-13-2016, 02:39 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-16T08:57:09-08:00","id":1003964,"title":"","content":"I personally think that Mage (and WW in general) would benefit a great deal from having more diversity of organizations or magical styles, supernatural beings, and so on. The lack of diversity was one thing that actually annoyed me about Mage when I first started learning about it. For a game about infinite possibilities and worldviews, the settings default groupings seem too restrictive in my opinion.<br /> <br /> All that said, I believe that there would mostly be an independent craft of Onmyoji but there would be plenty that would also join one Tradition or another especially if they are Onmyoji that migrated to other countries. I personally like more Crafts than less and think it would add more to the setting if they are a group of their own that have their own agendas separate from the Ascension War. Although they would have an interesting if not hostile relationship with the Technocracy.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4178-stanlemon","name":"stanlemon","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"209"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-16T13:42:24-08:00","id":1004099,"title":"","content":"I agree that Mage was limited at the beginning, but I think it got a lot better over time. More Mage groups were created, and existing ones were gradually expanded to cover more nuanced and varied interpretations of the broad Mage concepts. All the Traditions got Factions, the Conventions got Methodologies, etc. And now we have M20's Focus system, which is very flexible in how a character can be constructed.<br /> <br /> It's just that, sometimes, the existence of the Traditions and Crafts had the side effect of creating blind spots. If you heard White Wolf talk, there weren't any magical groups in Central and South America that weren't Aztec, Maya, or Inca in origin, or some variation of this. Let alone any sort of syncretic faiths in that neck of the woods that aren't Voudon related.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-30T03:24:54-08:00","id":1010128,"title":"","content":"Donno where they went, but there is a pretty good fic about a modern occultist (hermetic leanings) SI - ing into Naruto setting and becoming an Onmyoji to fit into paradigm. It's not officially an MtA crossover, but very well could be about the Orphan with hermetic style dramatically failing a demon summoning and ending up in the strange new reality.<br /> <br /> What I'm getting to is that Omnyoji is a hermetic style high-magic originating in Japan, but based on the celestial bureaucracy of dragon wizards and practices of Shinto queens. There is quite a big room to use it both in Asia and US. Particularly when you take into account it's increased popularity in media (manga, anime).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":4340,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4340-warpwind","user":"Warpwind","date":". 02-21-2018, 02:02 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4340-warpwind","name":"Warpwind","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4340_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"608"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}